On Sun, 25 Feb 2007, Bagels wrote:

> As I said previously, I'm gathering material for a paper on NetHack and
> its community.  One of the things I'm finding that I'd like to focus on
> is the twin questions of spoilers and save-scumming.

As far as save-scumming is concerned, if a player has fun this way, then
it's a fine way to play the game. Of course, sacrificing the amulet on the
right altar in a save-scumming game (or an explore mode game, or a game
stuffed with wizard bones) does not count as having "beaten" NetHack.

It's a bit like playing chess against a particularly good chess program:
If you reserve the right to always take back suboptimal moves, you might
very well get a win, but you would not really claim to have "beaten" a
2300 ELO program.

The main arguments in rgrn about save-scumming seems to be whether it
increases a player's ability to survive in a standard game. I think it
might be useful when the player restores a game after making a single,
stupid mistake (such as running into lava at the Val quest, getting
drowned by a giant eel at the castle); but of course, explore mode would
be more suited for this task, and in any case if it becomes a habit to
cheat death, then the player does not learn.

I can understand the sentiment that playing online removes the temptation
of save-scumming. I play online only in the /dev/null tournament, but
there I appreciate that all players hunting for trophies have an even
playing field.


As far as spoilers are concerned, I consider myself a "spade": I always
try to find out the mechanics of a game and use the knowledge to beat the
game. I like that we have formulae and tables for nearly everything, and
when we do not, then I can simply read the source code to figure it out.

In the chess analogy, spoilers would amount to studying your opponent's
past games and consulting opening libraries. Of course it is more pure to
become better just by playing chess over and over again and learning
solely from your games, but it would take much more time. This would
require too much work for my taste.

